With the expansion of industrial automation, mesh networked control systems (MNCSs) are increasingly applied, in which multiple control loops communicate over a shared mesh network, enabling wider coverage and resilient connectivity. MNCSs usually contain two types of message flows, i.e., sensing (uplink) flows delivering sensing measurements of plant states to the controller, and actuation (downlink) flows delivering control commands to the actuators. Existing scheduling approaches for MNCSs generally schedule the sensing and actuation transmissions in an end-to-end (E2E) manner within each control period, constraining the E2E delays to be bounded by the control period in order to satisfy hard or weakly-hard real-time requirements. However, under the limited mesh network resources, completing both uplink and downlink transmissions of each control loop within every control period, or even partial periods, may result in excessive and unnecessary resource costs. In this paper, we propose a bi-phased uplink and downlink scheduling strategy for MNCSs, which divides the E2E communication into sensing and actuation phases, allowing them to occur across different control periods. The scheme explicitly accounts for both the intermediate delays introduced by the sensing phase and the overall E2E delays across the two phases. We first develop a switched control model of MNCS that considers both intermediate and E2E delays under the bi-phased scheduling scheme. We theoretically show that the optimal finite-horizon schedule achieves a bounded performance gap with respect to the optimal infinite-horizon schedule, and propose a finite-horizon scheduling strategy that minimizes the overall control cost, subject to stability and uplink-downlink dependence constraints. Finally, we develop a priority-based iterative heuristic algorithm satisfying these constraints. Extensive evaluation results demonstrate that the bi-phased scheduling achieves superior control performance under various network utilizations, compared with state-of-the-art approaches.